---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import CitationBlock from "../../../components/CitationBlock.astro";
import PageIntro from "../../../components/PageIntro.astro";
import ScholarlyMeta from "../../../components/ScholarlyMeta.astro";
import SectionBlock from "../../../components/SectionBlock.astro";
import UseCaseFit from "../../../components/UseCaseFit.astro";
import {
  createImplementationPublication,
  implementationExampleAnchorLinks,
} from "../../../content/implementation-examples";

const pageTitle = "Government public services: implementation comparison";
const pageDescription =
  "Benefits automation governed two ways: review after harm, or named rule owners, upheld challenges counted against the rule, an outside halt, and repair first.";
const permalink = "/use-cases/examples/public-services";
const anchorLinks = implementationExampleAnchorLinks.filter(
  (link) => link.href !== "#standard",
);
const publication = createImplementationPublication(
  permalink,
  "Published the public services implementation comparison.",
);
---

<BaseLayout
  title={pageTitle}
  description={pageDescription}
  publishedTime="2025-06-01T00:00:00.000Z"
>
  <PageIntro
    eyebrow="Implementation example"
    title={pageTitle}
    description={pageDescription}
    anchorLinks={anchorLinks}
  />

  {
    /* Standard governance had a thin section of its own after this one, a
       five-item list. It is the first paragraph here, since the two cases
       are what it misses. */
  }
  <SectionBlock
    id="overview"
    eyebrow="Overview"
    title="Standard governance, and where it breaks down"
    description="Public services can move faster than appeals, leaving residents without immediate recourse."
  >
    <p>
      Standard governance relies on regulatory sandbox pilots before full
      deployment, impact assessments with public comment windows, advisory
      ethics and oversight boards, accountability structures for policy owners,
      and monitoring dashboards with periodic audits. That oversight often
      arrives after the harm is done.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two scored cases show what it misses.
      <a href="/casebook/robodebt">Robodebt</a> treated a tribunal's repeated findings
      of unlawfulness as individual outcomes, not as evidence against the scheme.
      In the
      <a href="/casebook/toeslagenaffaire">Dutch childcare benefits scandal</a>,
      a fraud risk score was treated as a finding and its reasons were withheld
      from the people it flagged. Ethotechnics requires that an upheld challenge
      reach whoever owns the rule, that someone outside the operator can force a
      halt, and that restoration come before restart.
    </p>
  </SectionBlock>

  <SectionBlock
    id="ethotechnics"
    eyebrow="Ethotechnics implementation"
    title="What changes, and what to verify before launch"
    description="Each eligibility rule has an owner, and upheld challenges are counted against it."
    variant="alt"
  >
    <div class="stack-sm">
      <ul class="card__list">
        <li>
          Each eligibility or debt rule names the official who owns it and can
          change it.
        </li>
        <li>
          A resident who challenges a decision gets the reasons and an answer by
          a stated date. That is what <a href="/glossary/contestability"
            >contestability</a
          > means here.
        </li>
        <li>
          Each upheld challenge is counted against the rule that produced it.
          The count goes to the rule's owner, who must review the rule and
          record what changed.
        </li>
        <li>
          A body outside the agency, named in advance, can force a halt. A halt
          contains the harm. It does not fix the rule. <a
            href="/glossary/stoppability">Stoppability</a
          > drills test that the halt works.
        </li>
        <li>
          Before the rule runs again, wrongly cut benefits are restored and
          wrongly raised debts are withdrawn.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="muted">
        Before launch, publish each rule's owner and who can force a halt. Set a <a
          href="/explainers/time-to-halt">time-to-halt</a
        > target and a
        <a href="/glossary/time-to-restore">time-to-restore</a> target, document how
        benefits are restored and debts withdrawn, and publish the result of a halt
        drill.
      </p>
    </div>
  </SectionBlock>

  <UseCaseFit slug="public-services" />

  <div id="citations">
    <CitationBlock
      title={pageTitle}
      permalink={permalink}
      publication={publication}
      summaryLabel="Cite this implementation example"
    >
      <ScholarlyMeta bare publication={publication} permalink={permalink} />
    </CitationBlock>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
